Bigfloat header and rounding state in parallel runners
=======================================================

Run ./maxima-local --no-init --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=["rtest_parallel_precision"]);'
The appended 28 cases bring that registered file to 45 problems.

The existing six precision variables and the memoized *BFLOAT-HEADER* /
*BFLOAT-HEADER-PREC* pair must be captured from the same caller. FPROUND
returns an exponent adjustment through *M; its scratch also needs a private
binding in caller, worker and fallback runners. A worker-only environment
does not protect the calling runner or inherit nested state.

Scope checks cover inherited values, initially unbound *M, normal return,
errors and THROW, and caller/worker nesting. THROW checks cross the calling
runner's scope; they do not attempt a nonlocal exit into a different thread.
Expected fixture error text goes to a broadcast stream; the checks assert
the conditions and state, without concurrent writes to a shared string stream.
A public scheduler can give several items to one runner. Its probe restores
each item's scratch before the next one checks that runner's entry state;
the actual runner is not required to reset state between items.

Two distinct native workers install different valid header/precision pairs
and rounding adjustments. Rendezvous ensure both writes precede either read.
Their results must retain their own complete state. Both workers remain alive
until their reads finish; missing workers and setup errors have bounded waits
and cleanup joins. Threadless configurations run the portable numeric check.

Sixty-four generated precision/rational-input combinations use the existing
exact binary bigfloat decoder and rational error bound, independently of a
serial BFLOAT result. No arithmetic algorithm or old expected answer changes.
These checks address precision state; generated solution-parameter identities
and other shared state require separate validation.
